B: 337.6 ns per operation (median)
A needs 13.8% less time than B: B takes 1.16 times as long as A.
With 95% confidence the difference lies between 10.8% and 15.5% less time (B takes between 1.12 and 1.18 times as long as A).
Noise floor: 0.339%. Identical code measured against itself on this setup can look that different, so smaller differences mean nothing.
Neighbouring measurements are nearly independent (autocorrelation -0.47), so they were resampled one by one.

Verdict: RESOLVED. A is faster than B: the difference is real, as its interval excludes zero and it exceeds the noise floor.

Warnings:
  - the program holds 64 MB of live data, more than the caches of many machines; for data that size, where it lies in memory can shift the result by several points in ways the interval does not cover, and differently in the next process; run the comparison in several processes with the multiproc package

